Consumer Value

MenoMorph aims to reduce the confusion between receiving skin-analysis
results and deciding which products to consider.

Instead of showing a score alone, it connects current skin metrics to
visible screening explanations, ingredient-watchlist matches, routine
suggestions, and product prices.

Its menopause-aware framing and wellness logger make room for the user's
own experiences rather than treating skincare discovery as a generic
catalog search.

The selected-product total also makes the cost of a shortlist visible
before any purchase decision.

Retail Value

MenoMorph demonstrates a guided discovery journey:

Selfie → Skin insights → Product-screening explanations → Routine suggestions
→ Selected-product basket.

For beauty retailers, this illustrates how skin analysis could support a
more contextual catalog experience. Explanations next to product choices
give shoppers a clearer reason to consider or review an item, while routine
grouping connects individual products to a broader discovery journey.

The prototype could be extended with verified inventory, retailer links,
virtual try-on, and checkout integrations.

These are proposed benefits, not measured results. The project has not yet
demonstrated increased conversion, reduced returns, improved skin outcomes,
or clinical effectiveness.